154
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:29,000
So in this podcast and scout master's ship, in seven minutes or less,

155
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:33,000
the first of three installments about youth leader development,

156
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:38,000
And this follows a series of posts on the blog that we did last month.

157
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:41,000
Then we have some email questions to answer,

158
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
And that should be enough for this podcast.

159
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:44,000
So let's get started.

160
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:49,000
Scout master's ship in seven minutes or less.

161
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:00,000
I often hear from scout masters who say something like: you know,

162
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:06,000
we train these boys, or we sent them to training and they still just don't seem to get the leadership thing.

163
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:12,000
And at this point a lot of us may grow disenchanted and conclude that a youth led troop

164
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:16,000
is just impossible or just too much trouble.

165
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:20,000
So scout youth leader training is very important.

166
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,000
We do a lot of training and we've got all kinds of initials for it.

167
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:29,000
We have NYLT and JLT and IOLST and all kinds of things.

168
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:33,000
Some approaches just work better than others, in my experience at least,

169
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
And here's what I've found-

170
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:41,000
Scout youth leaders who take part in event-based training don't automatically become leaders,

171
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:45,000
any more than anyone who spends a weekend in their garage becomes a car.

172
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:49,000
They may get some good out of it, but it's only the beginning of an ongoing process.

173
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:52,000
Now, what am I calling event-based training?

174
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:56,000
That's where we set aside a weekend or we send them off to a course of some kind,

175
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:00,000
and you know it lasts for two days or three days or two weekends or whatever,

176
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:06,000
And at the end of that we hand them the patch that says trained right,

177
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:10,000
Or we stamp them trained and we think, okay, that's it,

178
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
We've got them trained.

179
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:18,000
But I want to introduce the idea of development versus training.

180
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:23,000
Now, it may seem insignificant that I've swapped the term development for training,

181
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:29,000
but youth leader development seems to be a more fitting term than youth leader training in my book.

182
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:34,000
Training is something that has kind of a beginning, a middle and an end.

183
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:35,000
It's an event

184
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:39,000
And, like I said, we have a patch for it, or we did at least some time in the past

185
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:43,000
And everybody who participated in an event got to wear the trained patch.

186
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:47,000
I'd rather call training orientation,

187
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:52,000
And orientation is important, but it's only the beginning of this development process.

188
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
Development is ongoing,

189
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:56,000
It's perpetually unfinished,

190
00:11:56,000 --> 00:12:01,000
It moves from stage to stage and there's really no conclusion to the progression.

191
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:02,000
Development is a journey

192
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:05,000
And, like I said, it's a process.

193
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
Let's get started off on the right foot.

194
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:14,000
Scouts need an orientation to their leadership position, but then they need to develop

195
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:20,000
And to get started on the right foot. we need to have a good attitude of what the relationship is

196
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:28,000
between adults who are volunteering with their troop and scouts who have the responsibility to lead the troop.

197
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:31,000
Scouts are not employees.

198
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:40,000
Now it's natural for adult leaders to take the familiar terms and practices of our professional lives into scouting,

199
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:46,000
Because the relationships of the business world and the relationships of scouting are fundamentally different.

200
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:53,000
these kind of businesses or human resources practices are not going to improve the experience for us or our scouts.

201
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:56,000
Scoutmasters are not employers.

202
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
Scouts are not employees.

203
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:01,000
Their leadership position is not a job.

204
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:06,000
We're all voluntary participants in something much more interesting and complex.

205
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:17,000
Applications and contracts and job descriptions and quarterly reviews and various other human resources trappings have very limited usefulness in scouting.

206
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:24,000
Leaders develop by leading, not by studying leadership.

207
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:30,000
The way we train often gets in the way of how leaders develop.

208
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:35,000
We tend to over explain and over instruct and that's just plain frustrating to boys.

209
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:39,000
The most effective way to learn to tie a square knot is how,

210
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:42,000
By holding the rope and giving it a try,

211
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:46,000
The most effective way to develop as a leader is by leading.

212
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:54,000
Leadership development, then, is not preparation for leadership, but something that happens as you lead.

213
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:58,000
Leadership development is not a preparation for leadership.

214
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:01,000
It's not what we do ahead of time.

215
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
That let's call orientation,

216
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:09,000
That few minutes where we sit down and we say: do you understand the responsibilities that you've taken on with this position

217
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
And have you looked them up?

218
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:15,000
Have you read in your scout handbook or the senior patrol leader's handbook about this?

219
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:22,000
So development is the ongoing process that happens after they've been oriented and now they're leading.

220
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:27,000
Development is something that happens as they're leading.

221
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:32,000
Are the scouts in a completely boy-led troop more talented or effective?

222
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:36,000
Are they more energized than any other scouts?

223
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:37,000
Not really.

224
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:40,000
Some in my experience have been.

225
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:43,000
Some are very talented, very effective, very energized,

226
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:48,000
But for the most part they're pretty much boys like your scouts are

227
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:54,000
In a scout-led troop. are the boys who are participating in that troop somehow fundamentally different from other scouts?

228
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:55,000
No,

229
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:58,000
They're just boys, just the same way that your boys are.

230
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:05,000
The difference between scouts in a youth-led troop and scouts where adults are doing most of the leadership is simple.

231
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:10,000
The scouts in the youth-led troop are allowed to actually lead

232
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:14,000
And their development is seen as a process that happens over time

233
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:24,000
And we're going to talk in the next installment in this series about how to apply the idea of leadership development effectively.

234
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:31,000
He's my favorite all-time boy scout.

