You're my favorite all-time boy scout.

237
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:48,000
You're my favorite all-time boy scout.

238
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:51,000
You are always on the beat boy, beat boy.

239
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:55,000
You are always on the beat boy, beat boy.

240
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:58,000
I'll hang it in the street boy, street boy.

241
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:02,000
We will dance into the beat boy, beat boy. yeah,

242
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:05,000
You're my favorite all-time boy scout.

243
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:09,000
You're my favorite all-time boy scout.

244
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:13,000
He's my favorite all-time boy scout.

245
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:19,000
This is Cliff Jacobson and you're listening to the Scout Master Podcast with Clark Green.

246
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:24,000
Find me a letter, send it by name

247
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
Email. that is folks.

248
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:31,000
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

249
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:42,000
So this email comes from Doug Marks, who asked this question.

250
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:48,000
He says: you mentioned before a special designation for scouts who are over 18 and going to college.

251
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:52,000
You exempted them from certain training, not youth protection training.

252
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
Thanks for your great site.

253
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:57,000
Well, Doug, thanks for being in touch and asking the question.

254
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:01,000
The designation you're looking for is a membership code.

255
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:06,000
Now, everybody who registers as an adult leader in scouting is identified through a membership code,

256
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
and there's just plenty of them.

257
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:14,000
But the one that we're concerned with here is called the Unit College Scouter Reserve.

258
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
So this is a scout who's turned 18..

259
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:19,000
He's away at college or on a mission or in the service

260
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
And he wants to stay registered with the unit.

261
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:24,000
So when that situation occurs- and it occurs quite often-

262
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:28,000
the choices were either to register them as an assistant scoutmaster

263
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:32,000
or to register them with the council or to drop them.

264
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:36,000
Each of these choices had potential problems.

265
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
Assistant scoutmasters need to be trained

266
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:41,000
and that could be difficult at school or overseas,

267
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:46,000
And the College Scouter Reserve meant that they were not registered with the unit.

268
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:51,000
So dropping them from the charter often resulted in just plain losing contact with them.

269
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:57,000
To keep these young men involved in scouting, a new registration code was introduced

270
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:01,000
And the code, if you need to know it, is 9-2-U,

271
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:04,000
And that is the Unit College Scouter Reserve.

272
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:08,000
Of course, youth protection training is required for somebody

273
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:12,000
who is registered through the Unit College Scouter Reserve designation,

274
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:16,000
but that is the only required course for the position.

275
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:23,000
All of the registration application criteria and fees that apply to other leaders

276
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:26,000
apply to the Unit College Scouter Reserve.

277
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:32,000
I'll have a link in the post that contains this podcast to this information on scoutingorg.

278
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:36,000
So, Doug, I hope that answers your question, and it's a fairly frequent one.

279
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:41,000
but that's what it is. It's the Unit College Scouter Reserve,

280
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:44,000
The code is 9-2-U

281
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:47,000
And if you're confused about this or you need to answer questions,

282
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
get on the phone with your council registrar

283
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:52,000
and I'm sure they'll be able to help you figure everything out.

284
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:57,000
This next question- I'm going to withhold the name and the location and things like this

285
00:18:57,000 --> 00:19:00,000
because, well, you'll see, alright,

286
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
And it says this:

287
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:08,000
the reason I'm writing you is: one, I respect your views after many years of reading your blog,

288
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:13,000
and two, many times you make a point that I just wouldn't have considered.

289
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:16,000
We just had a great weekend camping with the troop,

290
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:21,000
but I have a scout who's been very disrespectful to me over the last several outings.

291
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:24,000
I've taken the high road, as I feel a scout master should,

292
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:27,000
And I've discussed this with the boys' father and the scout

293
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:33,000
on how this is unacceptable and try to get at the root of why it's happening.

294
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:36,000
The last incident happened in front of the whole troop.

295
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:39,000
I tried once again to talk to the scout

296
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:42,000
and he told me that he quit, and he didn't do it very elegantly

297
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:46,000
and he swore in the process and it was very upsetting.

298
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:51,000
So I tried to do everything right. but why is this bothering me so much?

299
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:54,000
Am I looking to you, for you did the right thing?

300
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:58,000
Well, maybe, but what I'm really looking for is one of those Clark points

301
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
I never considered. if there is one.

302
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:03,000
Well, I'm sorry to hear that this happened,

303
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:08,000
and usually when we have problems or troubles in scouting,

304
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:15,000
we can be pretty sure that a number of other people are having the same problems or troubles.

305
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:20,000
You know, we only know. a lot of times, all as we know is what's happening in our troop

306
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
or what's happening with a specific scout,

307
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:25,000
and it seems to be a very big thing,

308
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:29,000
And you know it is because it's our guys.

309
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:33,000
but a lot of people are going to run into this type of problem.

310
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:36,000
If you're a scoutmaster, you put yourself in the position

311
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:40,000
where you're going to run into these types of problems,

312
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:43,000
And I think the reason that this bothers you so much

313
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:45,000
is because you're basically a good guy.

314
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:49,000
You try hard. In this particular case you just could not win,

315
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
And there's a hard fact behind that.

316
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:54,000
We won't win with every scout.

317
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:57,000
We won't keep every scout involved.

318
00:20:57,000 --> 00:21:00,000
Sometimes we are going to lose.

319
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:05,000
Sometimes parents lose this battle with their own children too.

320
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:09,000
I've had a similar incident where a scout left throwing an invective

321
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:12,000
over his shoulder at me and it really hurt

322
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
And it still hurts.

323
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
He hasn't come back. He probably won't.

324
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:19,000
I tried to talk with him but he's not interested.

325
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:21,000
I've talked with his dad,

326
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
Tried to smooth everything out,

327
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:26,000
but I think that we lost this scout.

328
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:30,000
You've really got no idea what's happening to a boy

329
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:34,000
outside of what you can see in the context of scouting.

330
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:37,000
I mean, really who knows what he's dealing with?

331
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,000
The boy that walked away from my troop

332
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
was having some difficulties at home

333
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:44,000
and he had been for a year or two

334
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
And he had other things going on in his life.

335
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:49,000
I became the target of his anger and frustration.

336
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:53,000
I gave him some pretty good reasons to do that. frankly,

337
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:55,000
I just happened to be there at that time

338
00:21:55,000 --> 00:22:00,000
and he decided that I wasn't going to be a problem for him anymore.

339
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
That was his decision

340
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
And he's old enough to make that decision

341
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
Now. maybe he'll regret it someday, maybe not,

342
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:07,000
but it hurt me at the time

343
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:09,000
and you know, frankly, it still bothers me.

344
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,000
So did you do the right thing?

345
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:13,000
We may never know,

346
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:17,000
We don't always do the right thing.

347
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,000
We just do what we do

348
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:22,000
and we try really, really hard to get it right.

349
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:26,000
We have to face that sometimes we will not get it right,

350
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:29,000
and then we have to learn from it and move on.

351
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:31,000
Now, the thing that you might be missing here-

352
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:36,000
the secret Clark point that you're looking for-

353
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,000
is the fact that this is bothering you.

354
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:42,000
paradoxically, is the best thing about the situation.

355
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:46,000
If you were just a soulless, hard-nosed kind of a man

356
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
that justified every action you took

357
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
and slept easily when this sort of thing happened,

358
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
I'd tell you that you needed to do something

359
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
other than being a scoutmaster,

360
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:57,000
like, maybe, being a drill sergeant or something like that.

361
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:59,000
But you aren't that guy.

362
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:02,000
You care and you truly want to help.

363
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
You have a soft heart and it can be hurt,

364
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
and this is good.

365
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
It points to things in your character

366
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
that indicate you're the kind of a man

367
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:13,000
we want to have as a scoutmaster.

368
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:15,000
You can't win them all. when you don't,

369
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:17,000
The antidote is to look at all the other scouts

370
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:19,000
that are still there

371
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:21,000
and to do your best to help them.

372
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:23,000
So I hope that helps.

373
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:25,000
We've all found ourselves in difficult situations.

374
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:28,000
If you haven't found yourself in that kind of a difficult situation

375
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
with a scout, you will someday,

376
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:33,000
If you hang in there long enough, I guarantee it.

377
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:36,000
And remember: the most important thing for us to do

378
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:39,000
is to keep developing as good people

379
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:42,000
and to try and get things right,

380
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:44,000
And when we don't,

381
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:46,000
we have to be just as patient as ourselves,

382
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:49,000
as we are with the boys who don't get things right, sometimes.

